This chapter is mainly about what an operating system is, how to choose one, and the different types of Linux distributions and where they are used.

The main operating systems discussed are Windows, macOS and Linux. When choosing an operating system, organisations consider its role, function, lifecycle, stability, compatibility, cost and interface.

I learnt that desktop systems mainly use a GUI, while servers often use a CLI because they are usually managed remotely.

Linux is technically a kernel, while a Linux distribution combines the kernel with utilities, applications and management tools. Examples include Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, Red Hat, SUSE and Linux Mint.

Linux can be used through a GUI or CLI. The terminal allows users to interact with a shell, which interprets commands and passes them to the operating system.

 I also learnt about virtualisation and cloud computing, which help organisations reduce infrastructure costs and simplify management.

Finally, Linux is widely used in embedded systems, such as smartphones, smart TVs, Raspberry Pi devices, IoT devices and industrial systems.

What I learnt

I learnt what an operating system does, the difference between desktop and server systems, how GUI and CLI work, what Linux and Linux distributions are, the difference between a terminal and shell, and how Linux is used in servers, cloud environments and embedded systems.
